<worksheet xml:id="ws-gamma">
    <title>The Exponential, Gamma, and Chi-Square Distributions (3.2)</title>
    <page>
<exercise workspace=".75in">
  <statement>
     <p>
      Suppose that on average 6 people come to the DMV per hour according to a
      Poisson process. Find the probability that we wait more than 10 minutes
      for the first person to arrive.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>
   

    <p>
      If we ask the question <q>What is the waiting time until the first change
      in a Poisson process</q>? that is inherently a question answered by a
      <em>continuous</em> random variable <m>W</m> since the wait time can take
      on any value in the interval <m>(0,\infty).</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      We will develop the pdf for such a <m>W</m> by first finding the
      cumulative distribution function <m>F(w)</m> and then using the
      relationship that <m>F'(w)=f(w).</m>
    </p>
