 <page>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
     <p>
      Back to our example. Suppose on average <m>6</m> people come to the DMV
      per hour according to a Poisson process. Find the probability that we wait
      more than 10 minutes for the first person to arrive.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>X</m> be the wait time until the first person arrives. We expect on
      average 6 people per hour so we'd expect the first to arrive after 10 minutes.</p>
      <p>
      Thus <m>X</m> is an exponential random variable with parameter
      <m>\theta=</m><fillin characters="20"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace=".75in">
    <statement>
      <p>
            What is the pdf of <m>X</m>?
        
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
         Find <m>P(X\gt 10)</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>


<p>
     <em>Summary for Exponential</em>
    </p>

    <p>
      In an (approximate) Poisson process with mean <m>\lambda</m>, let <m>X</m>
      be the continuous R.V. that measures the waiting time until the first
      occurrence. 
    </p>
    <p>Then <m>X</m> has an <alert>exponential distribution</alert>
      with parameter <m>\theta = \frac{1}{\lambda}</m>. Further,
      <md>
        f(x) = \frac{1}{\theta}e^{-x/\theta}, \,\,\, 0\leq x \lt \infty
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>\mu=</m><fillin characters="20"/>,<m>\,\,\, \sigma^{2}=</m><fillin characters="20"/>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
        F(x)=P(X\leq x)=</m><fillin characters="20"/>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>
        P(X \gt x) = </m><fillin characters="20"/>
     
    </p>

    <p>
      Note for a continuous random variable, <m>P(X=x)=0</m> so
      <m>P(X\leq x)=P(X \lt x)</m> and <m>P(X \geq x)=P(X\gt x).</m>
    </p>
</page>
<page>

    <p>
      The exponential distribution (waiting time until first occurrence) might
      remind you in some ways of the geometric distribution where we were
      interested in the number of trials until our first success. Note the
      geometric distribution is a special case of the negative binomial
      distribution where we asked the number of trials until the <m>rth</m>
      success. The analog here would be to ask the waiting time until the
      <m>\alpha^{th}</m> occurrence. Again we will proceed by considering the
      cdf.
    </p>
