</exercise>

    <p>
      Still assuming <m>w\gt 0</m> we can find the pdf by looking at the
      derivative (using the chain rule and the product rule we get):
      <md>
        F'(w)= \lambda e^{-\lambda w}-e^{-\lambda w}\sum_{k=1}^{\alpha -1}\left[\frac{k(\lambda w)^{k-1}\lambda}{k!}-\frac{(\lambda w)^{k} \lambda}{k!}\right]
      </md>
      <md>
        =\lambda e^{-\lambda w}-e^{-\lambda w}\left[ \frac{1\cdot (\lambda w)^{0} \lambda}{1!}-\frac{(\lambda w)^{1} \lambda}{1!}+\frac{2(\lambda w)^{1} \lambda}{2!}-\frac{(\lambda w)^{2} \lambda}{2!}+\cdots -\frac{(\lambda w)^{\alpha -1}\lambda}{(\alpha-1)!}\right]
      </md>
    </p>
</page>
<page> 
    <p>
      <md>
        =\lambda e^{-\lambda w}-e^{-\lambda w}\left[ \lambda-\frac{(\lambda w)^{1} \lambda}{1!}+\frac{(\lambda w)^{1} \lambda}{1!}-\frac{(\lambda w)^{2} \lambda}{2!}+\frac{(\lambda w)^{2} \lambda}{2!}-+\cdots -\frac{(\lambda w)^{\alpha -1}\lambda}{(\alpha-1)!}\right]
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        =\lambda e^{-\lambda w}-e^{-\lambda w}\left[ \lambda -\frac{(\lambda w)^{\alpha -1}\lambda}{(\alpha-1)!}\right]
      </md>
      <md>
        =\frac{\lambda(\lambda w)^{\alpha -1}}{(\alpha-1)!}e^{-\lambda w}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>W</m> is said to have a <fillin characters="25"/> distribution.
    </p>
