<page>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        In summary, suppose we have a Poisson process with mean <m>\lambda</m>. If
      we let <m>X</m> be the continuous R.V. that measures the waiting time
      until the <m>\alpha</m>th occurrence then <m>X</m> has a
      <alert>gamma distribution</alert> with parameter
      <m>\theta = \frac{1}{\lambda}</m>. Note that since <m>\lambda</m>
      represents the average number of occurrence per unit of time, then
      <m>\theta</m> represents the expected wait time until the first
      occurrence. The pdf of <m>X</m> is
      
      <md>
        f(x)=\frac{1}{\Gamma(\alpha)\theta^{\alpha}}x^{\alpha -1}e^{-x/\theta}, \,\, 0 \leq x \lt \infty
      </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace=".5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <em>if <m>\alpha</m> is a positive integer</em> then
      <md>
        (cdf) F(x)=P(X\leq x)=1-\sum_{k=0}^{\alpha -1}\frac{(\lambda x)^{k} e^{-kx}}{k!}=1-\sum_{k=0}^{\alpha -1}\frac{(x/\theta)^{k} e^{-x/\theta}}{k!}
      </md>
      <m>=</m>
    </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace=".25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Further,  <m>\displaystyle{P(X\gt x)=\sum_{k=0}^{\alpha -1}\frac{(x/\theta)^{k} e^{-x/\theta}}{k!}}=</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>



    <p>
      It can be shown that the moment generating function, mean, and variance
      are as follows:
      <md>
        M(t)=\frac{1}{(1-\theta t)^{\alpha}}, \,\, t\lt \frac{1}{\theta}
      </md>
      <md>
        \mu = \alpha \theta, \,\, \sigma^{2}=\alpha \theta^{2}
      </md>
 
    </p>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
<p>
      Telephone calls enter a switchboard according to a Poisson process on
      average <m>2</m> every <m>3</m> minutes. Let <m>X</m> be the waiting time
      until you receive <m>3</m> calls.
    </p>
  </introduction>  
  <task workspace=".5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
          How is <m>X</m> distributed?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace=".75in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the probability that you have to wait no more than <m>10</m>
            minutes for the first <m>3</m> calls.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>
  
</page>
<page>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
       As in the last problem, suppose telephone calls enter a switchboard
            according to a Poisson process on average <m>2</m> every <m>3</m>
            minutes. Let <m>X</m> be the waiting time until the FIRST call
            arrives.
    </p>
  </introduction>
<task workspace=".75in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      How is <m>X</m> distributed?
    </p>
  </statement>
</task>
<task workspace="1in">
  <statement>
    <p>
       What is the probability that the first call comes within the
                  first minute?
    </p>
  </statement>
</task>
<task workspace="1in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      What is the probability that the first call doesn't come until
                  after 5 minutes?
    </p>
  </statement>
</task>
</exercise>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m>X</m> denote the number of particles emitted by a radioactive
            substance and recorded by a Geiger counter as clicks. Assume <m>X</m> follows
            a Poisson process with <m>\lambda=2</m> clicks per second. Let
            <m>W</m> be the waiting time to observe <m>4</m> clicks.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace=".75in">
    <statement>
      <p>
         What is the expected wait time (<m>\mu</m>)?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace=".75in">
    <statement>
      <p>
         Find the probability that you have to wait more than 5 seconds
                  to observe 4 clicks.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace=".75in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the probability that the first 4 clicks happen in the
                  first 2 seconds.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>
