 <page>

    <p>
      <em>A special case of the gamma distribution</em>
    </p>

    <p>
      Suppose <m>X</m> has a gamma distribution with <m>\theta=2</m> and
      <m>\alpha=r/2</m> where <m>r</m> is a positive integer. The pdf of
      <m>X</m> is then
      <md>
        f(x)=\frac{1}{\Gamma(r/2)2^{r/2}}x^{r/2-1}e^{-x/2}, \,\, 0\lt x\lt \infty
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      We say <m>X</m> has a <fillin characters="25"/> distribution with <fillin characters="40" />
    </p>

    <p>
      In this case we write
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>X \sim</m>
    </p>
    <p>
      We can't always use the sum formula for the cdf since <m>r/2</m> is not an
      integer (if <m>r</m> is odd) so the cdf for <m>X</m> is
      <md>
        F(x) = \int_{0}^{x}\frac{1}{\Gamma(r/2)2^{r/2}}w^{r/2-1}dw
      </md>
      and your calculator or table <m>IV</m> in your book can be used to find
      some values of <m>F(x)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <image width="80%" source="chisquare.png"/>
    </p>
</page>
<page>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
         Suppose <m>X \sim \chi^{2}(10)</m>. Use the tables to find the following,
      then use your calculator to verify. Note the lower bound if not given, is 0.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace=".25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>P(X \leq 20.48)=</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace=".5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
            <m>P(3.247 \leq X \leq 15.99)=</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
    
    <p>
      We will use this distribution more next term in statistics but
      for now we will just make a few more definitions and restrict our use to
      learning the notation and tables.
    </p>
<p> The <m>100p^{th}%</m> percentile (for <m>0\leq p \leq 1</m>) is a number <m>\pi_p</m> such that the area under the curve of <m>f(x)</m> to the left of <m>\pi_p</m> is equal to <m>p</m>. That is
<md>p=\int_{-\infty}^{\pi_p} f(x)\, dx = F(\pi_p)</md>
</p> 
    <p>
      Suppose <m>X</m> has a chi-square distribution with <m>r</m> degrees of
      freedom. Define <m>\chi^{2}_{\alpha}(r)</m> so that
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        P[X\geq \chi^{2}_{\alpha}(r)]=\alpha.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      In other words, the area under the curve of <m>f(x)</m> to the right of <m>\chi^{2}_{\alpha}(r)</m> is <m>\alpha</m>. It follows that <m>\chi^{2}_{\alpha}(r)</m> is the <m>100(1-\alpha)</m>th
      percentile.
    </p>

    
      
<exercise>
